---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import priorityEffects from '../figures/priority-effects.svg?raw';
import allocationTradeoff from '../figures/allocation-tradeoff.svg?raw';

const fukami = 'https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-ecolsys-110411-160340';
const goldford = 'https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aat1168';
const estrela = 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2021.09.011';
const scott = 'https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1192588';
const manhart = 'https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-05703-6';
const vannette = 'https://doi.org/10.1111/ele.12204';
const blount = 'https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0803151105';
const fukami2010 = 'https://web.stanford.edu/~fukamit/fukami-2010.pdf';
---

<Base title="Sebastian Correa-Gallego">
  <header class="hero">
    <img
      class="hero__img"
      src="/assets/field.webp"
      alt="Sebastian Correa-Gallego taking samples by headlamp in the Organal San Antonio, a volcaniclastic cave in Támesis, Colombia."
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <h1 class="hero__name">Sebastian<br />Correa-Gallego</h1>
        <p class="hero__degree">B.Sc. in Biology, Universidad EAFIT</p>
        <p class="hero__phrase">
          Microbial community assembly, and whether its outcome can be predicted from how a cell
          divides a finite proteome between growth rate and yield.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <a class="hero__cue" href="#question" aria-label="Continue to the question"><span aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
  </header>

  <section class="focus" id="question">
    <div class="wrap focus__inner">
      <h2 class="focus__lead reveal">
        When is community assembly reproducible, and when does arrival order decide the outcome?
      </h2>

      <div class="focus__grid">
        <div class="focus__text reveal">
          <p>
            Communities assembled from the same species pool under identical conditions sometimes
            converge on one reproducible state and sometimes diverge into several, depending on
            the order in which members arrive
            (<a href={fukami} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Fukami 2015</a>). Both regimes appear
            in the same experimental systems. Replicate glucose-limited communities converge at the
            family level while their species composition diverges, and that divergence traces to
            multistability in the population dynamics rather than to noise
            (<a href={goldford} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Goldford et al. 2018</a>,
            <a href={estrela} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Estrela et al. 2022</a>).
          </p>
          <p>
            Variation in growth traits alone is enough to produce more than one stable state on a
            single limiting resource
            (<a href={manhart} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Manhart and Shakhnovich 2018</a>),
            so the ingredients may be physiological. Ecology has candidate mechanisms for the
            history-dependent case, among them niche preemption, niche modification, and the size
            of the regional pool. What it does not have is a parameter, measured before assembly
            begins, that says which regime a given pool will fall into.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="focus__figure reveal">
          <Fragment set:html={priorityEffects} />
          <figcaption class="fig__caption">
            Two regimes over one species pool of nine, in three groups of three, with colour
            carrying the group. Each panel crosses three immigration histories against two habitat
            conditions. Above, assembly is deterministic and the community follows the habitat, so
            changing the history makes no difference. Below, it is historically contingent and the
            community follows the history instead, so changing the habitat makes none. After
            <a href={fukami2010} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Fukami 2010</a>, Fig. 4.1.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="focus" id="approach">
    <div class="wrap focus__inner">
      <h2 class="focus__lead reveal">
        Proteome allocation fixes each strain on a rate and yield axis, and can be measured
        before assembly begins.
      </h2>

      <div class="focus__grid">
        <div class="focus__text reveal">
          <p>
            Cells divide a finite proteome between ribosomes, which set growth rate, and metabolic
            enzymes, which set yield
            (<a href={scott} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Scott et al. 2010</a>). Traits of this
            kind already predict the strength of priority effects better than phylogenetic
            relatedness does, once they are separated into what a species requires and how it
            alters its environment
            (<a href={vannette} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Vannette and Fukami 2014</a>).
          </p>
          <p>
            The hypothesis is that the spread of allocation strategies across a pool predicts how
            far replicate communities diverge. A wide spread may strengthen priority effects, if
            early arrivals preempt resources that later ones cannot recover, or weaken them, if
            complementary strategies let late arrivals establish anyway. Permuting arrival order
            in synthetic communities built from characterised strains distinguishes the two.
            Microbes also evolve on the timescale of assembly, so whether adaptation amplifies
            contingency or erases it is the same question one level down
            (<a href={blount} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Blount et al. 2008</a>).
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="focus__figure reveal">
          <Fragment set:html={allocationTradeoff} />
          <figcaption class="fig__caption">
            The growth law on the left. The ribosomal share of the proteome rises in a straight
            line with growth rate and the metabolic share falls by the same amount, against a fixed
            housekeeping sector. On the right, the rate and yield tradeoff that partition implies.
            The same eight strains per pool are marked in both panels, and the bars give the range
            of growth rate each pool covers. The left panel follows the parameterisation of
            <a href={scott} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Scott et al. 2010</a>; the right is an
            inference from it, since yield is not measured there.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
